From df82f722eee803e9bd7bcb5060985d494eb97e73 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 19:13:11 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] LOANS-1: a capitalised moratorium's interest joins the debt
 at the first EMI

The projection showed the grown balance during a capitalised moratorium -
Rs 10L at 10% with 12 months capitalised is 10,00,000 x (1 + 1/120)^12 =
Rs 11,04,713.07 at repayment start, EMI Rs 14,598.86 over 120 months - but
nothing ever added that interest to the outstanding. The first EMI ended the
moratorium view, the live outstanding took over, and the loan read
Rs 9,93,734.47 owed with a new EMI of Rs 13,196.70: about Rs 1.05 lakh of debt
gone from net worth until somebody re-anchored to the lender.

The first instalment that repays principal now writes a dated row before it,
in the shape a re-anchor writes (a zero-amount charge whose principal is minus
the interest) plus a loan-account leg, so the outstanding, the loan account,
net worth, the EMI and the loan's ledger agree with the lender: Rs 9,205.94
interest and Rs 5,392.92 principal, Rs 10,99,320.15 owed, EMI unchanged. The
figure is the projection's own, the one the page showed until then.

It is added once: not for interest-only payments, serviced moratoriums or
loans without one, not after repayment has begun, and not when a re-anchor on
or before the instalment already holds the lender's balance. Undoing the
instalment takes the row and its leg with it; that undo is refused while a
later instalment or re-anchor stands on the grown balance. The leg marks the
row (a re-anchor posts none), so there is no migration. Existing loans past
their first EMI are not backfilled: no database seen holds a capitalised
moratorium, and a backfill could double-count a balance entered or
reconciled with the interest already in it. docs/loans-and-assets.md says so.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_016zXWwzys3K4XksLh3rrcKu
